Z||000002||fieroX||12-15-2001||03:29 AM||||first off the crankshaft is a forged piece and which is very strong (tested to 800 hp w/o failure) and the rods are cast, but awesome. If you look at the heritage of the L67 with the GN engines 15 yrs ago, the much more modern L67 blows it away internally.  The pistons contrary to popular belief are graphite impregnated cast hypereutectic.  Forged pistons for the L67 are very expensive, not to mention heavy.  I had my pistons thermal coated for nitrous use, and they will hold up fine.  I have heard on the Grand Prix forum of a guy that put a direct port nitrous system on his L67 shooting 480 hp wet shot (not a typo yes 480shot)  dynoed over 600 at the wheels, without any internal problems.  A broken flywheel though.<BR>I recommend if you run nitrous do work to your exhaust system.  Ported heads, headers, and a decent flowing exhaust.  Check my website over the next month, as I will be detailing head porting, header building, and custom exhaust.  (BTW not only will my engine make 350 hp at the crank no n2o, Im going to have a 100 direct port n2o system, JIC a ferrari wants to play!)<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
